# Reference output for the headless renderer.
#
# `--headless` renders on a fixed timestep with no terminal control, so a fixed
# seed produces the same bytes every time. Checking those bytes against a
# committed hash is what turns that property into a test with an opinion: the
# run-to-run comparison beside it only says a flight is repeatable, and a
# change that quietly alters every frame is repeatable too.
#
# Checked two ways, from one file. `cargo test --test golden` reproduces the
# bytes in process, so a renderer that has drifted fails on the machine that
# drifted it; the `headless` CI job produces the same files from a release
# binary and runs `sha256sum -c` over this, which also proves the two agree.
#
# Ten flights, sharing
#
#     --headless --frames 120 --seed 1 --size 120x36
#
# and differing in what they ask the renderer to draw:
#
#     truecolor.txt   --demo --color truecolor
#     ascii.txt       --demo --color ascii
#     ansi256.txt     --demo --color 256
#     warp.txt        --engage --throttle 1.0 --color truecolor
#     side.txt        --engage --throttle 1.0 --view side --color truecolor
#     orbit.txt       the same, --orbit 55,35,20
#     astern.txt      the same, --orbit -75,6,20
#     steer.txt       --demo --fps 10 --color truecolor
#     drift.txt       the same, --view side --orbit -60,0,0
#     ahead.txt       --engage --throttle 1.0 --fps 10 --view side --orbit 90,0,0
#
# All ten moved when the sky stopped being a box in front of the camera and
# became a volume the ship is inside, and most of what this block used to say
# about them went with it. What each case was *for* had been written down in
# terms of the star band's own machinery — the fast paths that reduced to
# identities when the camera was abeam, the sign of the depth travel, the ramp
# the near wall needed, the fold that carried a trail across the edge — and
# there is no band any more, so none of those reasons survives its subject.
#
# What the spread is for now is simpler and mostly unchanged: ten flights over
# one sky, at both speeds, from six camera angles, in three colour modes, with
# and without a hand on the stick. Read it as a grid rather than as a list of
# special cases.
#
#   Ramp.         Seven of the ten moved when a streak stopped ending at a
#                 third of its head's brightness and started running out at
#                 nothing. The three that did not are the 60 fps `--demo` runs,
#                 whose exposure is an exact zero: every streak is a point and
#                 the ramp is never evaluated at all.
#
#                 The floor was right while a streak was one step of motion —
#                 a tail was where the star had been a frame ago, and nothing
#                 ever left through it. A streak is an exposure seconds long
#                 now, so the tail is where the shutter closes and everything
#                 ageing out of the picture leaves through exactly that edge.
#                 A third of full brightness there is a bright edge dragged
#                 across the frame: hold a turn at low warp, where the whole
#                 trail is the smear the turn left, and the sky holds its
#                 streaks for a second after the stick comes back and is then
#                 wiped. One ramp, running out at nothing, is what makes a
#                 trail thin away as it ages.
#
#   Falloff.      Five of the ten moved when a streak stopped being measured by
#                 the window it is seen through and started being measured by
#                 the track it flew — `warp.txt`, `side.txt`, `astern.txt`,
#                 `steer.txt` and `ahead.txt`. What separates the five that
#                 moved from the five that did not is worth reading, because
#                 neither half is what it looks like. The three 60 fps `--demo`
#                 flights never leave sublight, so their exposure is an exact
#                 zero, every streak is a point and the falloff is never
#                 applied at all: those three are untouched by construction.
#                 `orbit.txt` and `drift.txt` are the interesting pair. The
#                 change reaches them — the same two flights at 300x90 come out
#                 wholly different — but at 120x36 it moves not one eight-bit
#                 level, because most of what the bubble reaches in an exterior
#                 frame already went through `draw_path`, which was measuring
#                 the whole streak all along. So they are not a control saying
#                 the change stayed put; they are two framings where it is too
#                 small to see. The property that says it happened is in
#                 `canvas.rs`, where two terminals of different widths are made
#                 to draw the same streak and compared.
#
#   Colour.       `truecolor.txt` and `ansi256.txt` are the same flight in two
#                 spellings, so a change that moves one and not the other has
#                 landed in the writer rather than in the renderer. Detection is
#                 gone and nothing is handed the palette mode by default, which
#                 makes `ansi256.txt` the only guard on `quantize_256` and the
#                 palette-index path. `ascii.txt` is the third, and the only one
#                 that says nothing outside printable ASCII was emitted.
#
#   Speed.        The five `--demo` runs and the five `--engage` ones split the
#                 sky's whole dynamic range, and that split is sharper than it
#                 used to be: at impulse the stars now hold *still* — a full
#                 impulse frame differs from a parked one only by the twinkle —
#                 while at warp the exposure draws three seconds of each star's
#                 own track. A change to the exposure moves the flights that
#                 light a drive and leaves the rest alone — seven of the ten
#                 now, and the count is measured rather than asserted: this
#                 block said five until something finally tested it. Pacing the
#                 exposure to the distance actually flown moved every
#                 `--engage` run and both `--fps 10` ones, whose drive lights
#                 at frame 60 of a twelve-second flight, while `truecolor.txt`,
#                 `ascii.txt` and `ansi256.txt` came out byte for byte
#                 identical: two seconds of `--demo` at 60 fps never leaves
#                 sublight, and at sublight the exposure is an exact zero.
#
#                 Lighting a drive is not the same as spooling one up, which is
#                 what `ahead.txt` is here to say. Every `--engage` flight but
#                 that one lasts two seconds, in which the ship covers only a
#                 few light years, so the exposure never reaches far enough for
#                 anything that depends on its *length* to happen at all. That
#                 is how a bug that lost the trail of every star inside the
#                 exposure — a fifth of the pool — moved not one of the nine
#                 hashes that existed before this case was added.
#
#   Camera.       Five flights watch from outside and they are at five
#                 different angles, which is now a question about which sky is
#                 in front of the lens rather than about which arithmetic is
#                 switched on. `side.txt` is abeam, where `Eye::to_camera` is an
#                 exact swizzle and the hull's axis lies flat in the image
#                 plane, so it is still the shot with no vanishing point at all
#                 and still the one the engine lance's clamp cannot move.
#
#                 What the angle decides for the *sky* is the sign of
#                 `Orbit::nose_in_camera`'s depth component,
#                 `-cos(elevation)·sin(azimuth)`: behind the ship the exposure
#                 recedes and ahead of it the exposure runs at the lens and has
#                 to be cut against the near plane. `astern.txt` holds one side
#                 of that and `ahead.txt` the other, at `--orbit 90,0,0` where
#                 the nose points straight down the barrel and the cut bites
#                 hardest. `orbit.txt` is nominally on the forward side too and
#                 cannot substitute: two seconds is not long enough an exposure
#                 for the cut to reach any star, which is the whole of why
#                 `ahead.txt` runs at `--fps 10`.
#
#   Stick.        There is no hand on the stick anywhere in this file, and
#                 there is now no way to put one here: nothing on the command
#                 line steers and a headless flight has no keyboard, so the
#                 autopilot was the only thing that ever did, and it flies
#                 straight. All ten hold every steering rate at an exact zero
#                 and `LEVEL_AXES` to the bit from the first frame to the last.
#
#                 So this is a *control* rather than a split — a change to how
#                 the sky is steered moves nothing here, and anything that does
#                 move for one has leaked. That is worth more than it sounds:
#                 the shape it replaces was four flights against four, and what
#                 it bought was the ability to say which side a change landed
#                 on. What the whole set buys now is the stronger half of that,
#                 which is that a deletion from the steering path being the
#                 identity it looks like is visible in ten byte-identical
#                 hashes rather than in six. `tests/golden.rs` says the same
#                 thing directly, by running each case's own autopilot and
#                 asking for the three rates, so an autopilot that picked the
#                 stick back up fails with a sentence rather than with five
#                 moved hashes and no account of them.
#
#                 What that costs has to be stated rather than discovered. A
#                 streak is the track a star swept while the shutter was open,
#                 so one the ship flew straight through is a segment and one it
#                 turned through is a curve — and no flight here draws the
#                 second kind. `Universe::stations`, the multi-leg walk through
#                 `Canvas::draw_path`, `Track::turn_over` and the near-plane cut
#                 on a tail a turn has swung are pinned by property tests in
#                 `universe.rs`, `track.rs` and `canvas.rs`, and by
#                 `a_turn_at_warp_can_be_flown_from_the_library_alone` in
#                 `tests/flight.rs` — and by nothing at all in this file. A
#                 change that repainted every turn ever flown would move not one
#                 hash here. Closing that honestly means a flight that steers,
#                 which means a way to steer one from a command line.
#
#   Shift.        Seven of the ten moved when the Doppler shift stopped being
#                 only a colour and became a brightness as well. The three that
#                 did not are the 60 fps `--demo` runs, whose warp ramp is an
#                 exact zero from the first frame to the last — `shift_light`
#                 returns exactly one there, `intensity · 1.0` is exact, and a
#                 sublight frame is the frame the drive's own arithmetic is not
#                 there for.
#
#                 That split had to be *measured on a build of its own*, and
#                 that is the thing to carry forward rather than the count. Two
#                 other changes landed in the same commit and both move all ten,
#                 so with the three of them in the tree together there is no
#                 byte-identical control left in this file to say the shift is
#                 warp-gated at all. Three builds said it instead: the shift on
#                 its own, the zero point on its own, and the reach's respelling
#                 on its own. Anything else that arrives here without a control
#                 wants the same treatment — a hash that cannot move is worth
#                 more than one that did, and a commit that takes the last one
#                 away owes the reader the build that had it.
#
#   Zero point.   All ten moved when the sky's baseline came down a fifth of a
#                 magnitude to make room for the shift above, and that is the
#                 shape rather than a leak: the dim is not gated on the drive,
#                 so a parked ship is dimmer too. It separates nothing at all,
#                 which is the reading — a change that moves ten this way is a
#                 change to the photometry rather than to any one view, and
#                 `hud.rs` is the other thing that moves ten (see "Panel.").
#                 Told apart by which files disagree: a panel edit shifts the
#                 same characters in every frame, and this moved no character
#                 in `ascii.txt`'s panel and most of its sky.
#
#   Reach.        Nine of the ten moved when `reach_sq` stopped being spelled as
#                 `lumen / faintest` and started being taken from the limit
#                 directly. The two are the same number on paper — the zero
#                 point cancels — and were not in `f32`, because each side went
#                 through its own `powf` and `10^x` turns an ulp on the exponent
#                 into a part in a million on the answer. So every reach moved
#                 by about 4e-7, and with it every `sqrt`, every radius and
#                 every star's place: 51% of a default seeded sky, measured.
#                 `ascii.txt` is the one that held, which is a fact about that
#                 mode's coarseness rather than about the change — sixteen
#                 glyphs of ramp do not resolve a part in a million, and the
#                 other two spellings of the same flight both moved.
#
#   Panel.        Five of the ten move for a change to the instrument panel,
#                 and which five is a fact about who is flying rather than
#                 about what is drawn: the `--engage` flights put glass in front
#                 of the frame and the `--demo` ones draw none, because a flight
#                 nobody is flying has no readings anybody asked for and no keys
#                 worth naming. It used to be all ten, and the point of the
#                 paragraph then was that it therefore separated *nothing* — an
#                 edit up there deleted or shifted the same characters in every
#                 file and told the ten apart not at all. It tells the two kinds
#                 of flight apart now, which is a shape rather than a wash.
#
#                 Two guards went with the other five and are worth knowing
#                 about before they are needed. `truecolor.txt` and
#                 `ansi256.txt` are still one flight in two spellings, but both
#                 are `--demo`, so the pair no longer says anything at all about
#                 a panel edit and a panel colour spelled wrong in the palette
#                 mode would pass here unremarked —
#                 `the_escapes_written_by_hand_are_the_ones_crossterm_writes` in
#                 `term.rs` is what is left of it. And `ascii.txt` carries no
#                 panel, so the `headless` job's grep sees the starfield's
#                 brightness ramp and nothing else; the panel's ASCII face is
#                 greped from a second `--engage --color ascii` frame piped
#                 straight into that step, and pinned properly by
#                 `the_ascii_panel_is_actually_ascii` in `hud.rs`.
#
#   Autopilot.    `drift.txt` shares every flag with `steer.txt` but the view,
#                 which makes the pair one flight watched from inside and from
#                 outside — the relationship `ansi256.txt` has to
#                 `truecolor.txt`, applied to a camera instead of to a colour
#                 mode. It is parked aft of the beam rather than on it so the
#                 camera *crosses* the beam mid-flight, at frame 72 with the
#                 drive lit since frame 60, which is the band the drive swaps
#                 sides over traversed as a ramp rather than sat beside.
#
#                 That angle is derived from `autopilot::CAMERA_TURN` and has to
#                 be re-derived whenever that moves: it is how far the camera
#                 walks in the seconds before the crossing should land, and the
#                 crossing has to land after the drive lights at frame 60. It
#                 was -60,0,0 at a turn every 43 seconds and -20,0,0 at every
#                 137.
#
#   Hands off.    Five of the ten moved when the autopilot stopped touching the
#                 stick and a flight nobody is flying stopped being given an
#                 instrument panel — the five `--demo` runs, with the five
#                 `--engage` ones byte for byte.
#
#                 Both halves of that change move the *same* five, so neither is
#                 a control for the other and this file could not have told them
#                 apart on its own. That is the trap the "Shift." note above
#                 describes, so they were shot on builds of their own, and the
#                 reading that separates them is which *rows* moved rather than
#                 which files. Taking the weave out moved all 36 rows of every
#                 frame it touched, which is the whole sky: the ship carries the
#                 attitude the sky is projected through, so a weave nobody asked
#                 for was swinging the entire field. Gating the panel moved
#                 nothing but the panel's own rows — 1 to 6, 15 and 21 in the
#                 cockpit flights, where the last two are the reticle, 1 to 7 in
#                 `drift.txt`, which has a `SHIP` row and no reticle, and 33, 34
#                 and 35 in all five. Not one sky row, which is what says the
#                 gate stayed in `hud.rs` and did not leak into the renderer.
#
#   Edge.         Four of the ten moved when `draw_path` stopped collapsing a
#                 sub-subpixel exposure to its head *before* anything had
#                 clipped — `side.txt`, `orbit.txt`, `astern.txt` and
#                 `drift.txt`, which is every exterior flight with a lit drive
#                 except `ahead.txt`. The clip is what decides whether a star is
#                 on screen at all, so a short exposure straddling the frame
#                 edge, head just off and tail just on, was a degenerate segment
#                 wholly outside the canvas and was dropped. `draw_streak`
#                 clipped the real thing and drew the end that survived. The
#                 star winked out at the boundary instead of sliding off it, and
#                 which of the two it winked out in depended on how many poses
#                 the exposure happened to have.
#
#                 The split is a statement about paths rather than about
#                 cameras: only a path of three points or more reaches
#                 `draw_path`'s own degenerate branch, and only the bent sky
#                 makes those. `ahead.txt` is the exterior flight that does not
#                 move, because its exposures are cut at the near plane and
#                 project far off-canvas rather than landing on the edge of it.
#
#   Compass.      Two of the ten moved when `heading` and `pitch` stopped being
#                 integrals of the steering rates and started being read off the
#                 attitude the ship is actually holding — `steer.txt` and
#                 `drift.txt`, and nothing else. That pair was the sharpest
#                 split this file had, because it separated on what a flight
#                 *did* rather than on where it was watched from: they were the
#                 two twelve-second `--demo` runs, and the ones with the most
#                 stick in them. It separates nothing now — no flight steers, so
#                 the attitude is `LEVEL_AXES` in all ten and both readings are
#                 an exact zero everywhere. Kept because the *method* is the
#                 thing worth carrying forward, and because the sentence that
#                 stood here about the 60 fps runs stopping "four seconds short
#                 of the weave" was already wrong: the weave had been widened to
#                 the whole cycle, and taking it out moved all three of them.
#
#                 The panel is stamped into the character grid after `compose`,
#                 so it is inside the pinned bytes — and every differing line in
#                 both files is a `HEADING` row, with none that is not. An edit
#                 to an instrument that moved a streak would say so here.
#
# None of the ten names a sky, so all of them fly the default limiting
# magnitude — which is a fixed number of stars over the whole celestial sphere
# on every terminal, where `--stars` was a fixed number *drawn*. The reference
# is also still one ship deep: no case passes `--ship`, so all five flights
# with a hull in them fly the enterprise, and a fault that only some other
# outline exposes wants a property test over `models()` rather than an eleventh
# flight.
#
# The hashes hold across build profiles and rustc versions but not across
# platforms: `sin`, `cos`, `exp` and `powf` come from the system maths library,
# and recycling the sky turns a one-ulp difference into a different universe.
# So they are checked on `ubuntu-latest` only, and `tests/golden.rs` is gated to
# Linux for the same reason.
#
# Regenerating: build a release binary and run the ten commands in `CLAUDE.md`,
# then put this block back. Say in the commit message which moved and why.
